---
// Proof-point stat band. Every number names the tier it was measured at:
// this page is a transparency exercise, not a sales page.
---

<section class="cql-stats">
  <div class="cql-container">
    <div class="cql-stats__grid">
      <div class="cql-stat">
        <div class="cql-stat__top">
          <div class="cql-stat__value">~4%</div>
          <span class="cql-stat__tier">at T1</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cql-stat__caption">
          average gap between our offline quote and what the query actually costs to run, on a public API we don't run, with zero server changes
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cql-stat cql-stat--accent">
        <div class="cql-stat__top">
          <div class="cql-stat__value">11%</div>
          <span class="cql-stat__tier">at T2</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cql-stat__caption">
          the API times each field, so work that runs at the same time can't hide inside the
          stopwatch. That put our quote within 11% of the real cost on the movie API.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cql-stat">
        <div class="cql-stat__top">
          <div class="cql-stat__value">38.5×</div>
          <span class="cql-stat__tier">at T3</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cql-stat__caption">
          our test database was asked for the same data 963 times, but really ran only 25
          queries. costQL saw that and priced the 25, not the 963.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="cql-stats__note">
      T1, T2, and T3 are costQL's <a href="#tiers">three depths of fidelity</a>: you pick
      the one your needs justify.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
